Pouch: musl's raw opens through openat's translation; __map_file maps private.

internal/syscall.h's sys_open family takes musl's SYS_open arm whenever
SYS_open is defined, and Pouch defines it as 65, the native open, whose
arguments are (start_fd, path, length, omode), not Linux's (path, flags,
mode). So __map_file's open sent the path pointer as the start descriptor and
the flags as the path, the kernel refused it, and every time zone file, locale
file and message catalog (tzset, MUSL_LOCPATH, catopen, gettext) failed to
load, softly: UTC, the C locale, untranslated messages. Patch 0023 said the
macro reached the 0xFFFF SYS_openat sentinel and routed the stdio openers
through open(); it reached SYS_open, and __map_file was not among them.

The four macros now call __pouch_sys_open, openat's body, which answers a
descriptor or -errno and leaves errno alone; openat() sets errno from it. The
openers 0023 rerouted (fopen, __fopen_rb_ca, tmpfile) go back to upstream's
sys_open, so those stdio functions no longer call the POSIX name open().

Behind the open, __map_file asked for MAP_SHARED, which Pouch's mmap refuses
with ENOSYS (no shared file mapping exists, ARCHITECTURE.md 6.5). It now maps
MAP_PRIVATE and read-only, all its readers need. The kernel rounds the length
up to a page and serves the partial last page; munmap rounds the same way.

